-- THE CATALOG'S FIRST ROWS — the live-verified servers this install starts knowing about,
-- carried over from the list that used to live in code. Each becomes one global `mcp_server`
-- (workspace_id null) plus ONE published revision holding the document it stands for, in the
-- official registry format.
--
-- The editorial half travels with them and is the point: `auth_mode` is what a live check of the
-- vendor's own discovery chain concluded, not what the vendor claims, and every `manual` row
-- carries the one line saying what the person has to do first. The same word also rides the
-- document's `_meta`, so a machine reading the bytes and a person reading the row are told the
-- same thing.
--
-- Ids are DETERMINISTIC here — `mcps_`/`mcpr_` over the md5 of the row's own slug — so this file
-- is re-readable and re-runnable. They carry the SHAPE every minted id carries (the prefix and 32
-- lowercase hex), because a revision id travels on the wire: a machine reports back the revision it
-- holds, and a handwritten id of some other shape would be refused at the door that receives it.
-- Everything minted later is random, like every other id in this schema.
--
-- The pointer move is its own statement at the end: a server's `current_revision_id` cannot name
-- a revision that does not exist yet, and a revision cannot name a server that does not either.

